<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">London Protocol (1830)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Protocol of 3 February 1830 was the agreement by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> as an independent sovereign state under a hereditary monarch, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a>. It set a narrow northern frontier, later widened, and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> outside the new kingdom. The throne was eventually given to Otto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1832.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london-protocol-1830.jpg" alt="London Protocol (1830)" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ludwig Michael von Schwanthaler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A0%CF%81%CF%89%CF%84%CE%BF%CE%BA%CE%BF%CE%BB%CE%BB%CE%BF_%CE%B1%CE%BD%CE%B5%CE%BE%CE%B1%CF%81%CF%84%CE%B7%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Greek historiography calls the agreement the Protocol of Independence. The Greek War of Independence had begun in 1821, and the British Foreign Secretary George Canning wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> recognised in order to check <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s expansion into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. The Protocol of St Petersburg of April 1826 first recognised Greek autonomy, and the Treaty of London of July 1827 proposed a Greece paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino">Battle of Navarino</a>. A first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> protocol of November 1828 offered Greece only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a>; a second, of March 1829, drew a border from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagasetic-gulf">Pagasetic Gulf</a> but kept Ottoman sovereignty and an annual tribute.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and imposed the Treaty of Adrianople in 1829, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> came to favour an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> that would block Russian influence, though it wanted the western border kept well away from the Ionian Islands it then occupied.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The protocol was signed by Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>, Montmorency-Laval and Lieven, with no Greek or Turkish representatives present; Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-kapodistrias">Ioannis Kapodistrias</a> was excluded on British suspicion that he was stirring revolt in the Ionian Islands. Its 11 articles drew the border on the Aspropotamos–Spercheios line, from the mouth of the Achelous to that of the Spercheios, and gave Greece Euboea, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and the Sporades. A second protocol that day chose Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium">Leopold of Saxe-Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> as Ruler Sovereign of Greece with a loan for his army, and the powers required Greece to respect the lives and property of Muslims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> accepted Greek independence, while Kapodistrias pressed Leopold to seek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and Psara. Refused funds by the British government, Leopold withdrew. A protocol of September 1831 extended the frontier to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagasetic-gulf">Pagasetic Gulf</a> line, but days later, on 9 October 1831, Kapodistrias was assassinated by Konstantis and Georgios Mavromichalis. The powers then named the 17-year-old Prince Otto of Bavaria King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and he arrived with a Regency Council in 1833.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “London Protocol (1830)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>London Protocol</b> of 1830 recognised an independent Greek state, but its first governor, Ioannis Kapodistrias, was assassinated in 1831 and the country fell into civil strife.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>London Protocol</b> of 1830 recognised Greek independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-hellenic-republic">First Hellenic Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Samos fought in the Greek War of Independence, but the <b>London Protocol</b> of 1830 left it outside the new Greek state.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-samos">Principality of Samos</a></li></ul></section>
